---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Navbar } from '../../components/layout/Navbar';
import Footer from '../../components/layout/Footer.astro';
import Badge from '../../components/ui/Badge.astro';
import BlogCard from '../../components/ui/BlogCard.astro';
import CTABanner from '../../components/sections/CTABanner.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';

const navLinks = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'About', href: '/about' },
  { label: 'Services', href: '/services' },
  { label: 'Blog', href: '/blog' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '/contact' },
];

const relatedPosts = [
  {
    title: '10 UX Principles Every Designer Should Know',
    excerpt: 'A comprehensive guide to the foundational psychological principles that drive intuitive and accessible user interfaces.',
    category: 'Design',
    date: 'March 05, 2024',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    author: { name: 'Elena Rodriguez' },
    href: '#'
  },
  {
    title: 'Building Scalable APIs with Node.js',
    excerpt: 'Best practices for structuring your backend services to handle millions of requests without sacrificing performance.',
    category: 'Engineering',
    date: 'March 10, 2024',
    readTime: '12 min read',
    author: { name: 'Marcus Johnson' },
    href: '#'
  },
  {
    title: 'Introduction to Design Systems',
    excerpt: 'Why your growing team needs a single source of truth for UI components, and how to start building one today.',
    category: 'Design',
    date: 'February 12, 2024',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    author: { name: 'Thomas Mueller' },
    href: '#'
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout themeSlug="ecommerce">
  <Navbar client:load logo="Brand" links={navLinks} ctaLabel="Get Started" />
  
  <main class="flex-1 bg-[var(--color-bg)]">
    <!-- Article Header -->
    <header class="pt-24 pb-12 md:pt-32 md:pb-16 bg-[var(--color-bg-subtle)] border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-4xl text-center">
        <Badge variant="primary" class="mb-6 px-3 py-1 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sm">Design</Badge>
        
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-extrabold text-[var(--color-text)] font-heading tracking-tight mb-8 leading-tight">
          The Future of AI in Product Design
        </h1>
        
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-6 text-[var(--color-text-subtle)] text-sm md:text-base">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-light)] text-[var(--color-primary-dark)] flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold font-heading shadow-sm">
              S
            </div>
            <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Sarah Chen</span>
          </div>
          <div class="hidden sm:block w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-[var(--color-border-strong)]"></div>
          <time datetime="2024-03-15">March 15, 2024</time>
          <div class="hidden sm:block w-1 h-1 rounded-full bg-[var(--color-border-strong)]"></div>
          <span class="flex items-center gap-1">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
            8 min read
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Hero Image Placeholder -->
    <div class="w-full max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 -mt-8 relative z-10">
      <div class="w-full h-[400px] md:h-[500px] bg-[var(--color-primary-light)] rounded-[var(--radius-2xl)] shadow-[var(--shadow-lg)]"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Content -->
    <article class="py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 max-w-[720px] prose prose-lg md:prose-xl prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:text-[var(--color-text)] prose-p:text-[var(--color-text)] prose-p:leading-relaxed prose-a:text-[var(--color-primary)] prose-strong:text-[var(--color-text)] prose-li:text-[var(--color-text)] prose-blockquote:border-l-[var(--color-primary)] prose-blockquote:text-[var(--color-text-subtle)] prose-blockquote:font-medium prose-blockquote:not-italic">
        
        <p>The integration of Artificial Intelligence into our daily workflows is no longer a distant sci-fi concept; it's happening right now. For product designers, this shift brings both tremendous opportunity and understandable anxiety. How will AI change the way we create? Will it replace us? The reality is far more nuanced, pointing towards a future where AI acts as a powerful co-pilot rather than a replacement.</p>

        <h2>The Rise of AI-Assisted Wireframing</h2>
        <p>One of the most immediate impacts we are seeing is in the ideation and wireframing stages. Historically, turning a product requirement into a set of mid-fidelity wireframes took days. Now, language models trained on vast repositories of UI patterns can generate initial structures in minutes.</p>
        <p>This doesn't mean the designer's job is done. Instead, the baseline has shifted. Designers can now start from a solid foundation, skipping the "blank canvas" phase entirely. This allows more time to focus on complex user flows, edge cases, and the unique brand elements that an AI cannot organically invent.</p>

        <h2>Generative UI and Adaptive Interfaces</h2>
        <p>Perhaps the most exciting frontier is Generative UI. Imagine an interface that isn't hard-coded but generated on the fly based on the user's specific context, permissions, and historical behavior.</p>
        
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Personalization at scale:</strong> UIs that adapt their density based on a user's technical proficiency.</li>
          <li><strong>Accessibility first:</strong> Layouts that automatically restructure themselves for better readability depending on the device and lighting conditions.</li>
          <li><strong>Contextual relevance:</strong> Surfacing tools and actions only when the system predicts they are needed.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Designing for Generative UI requires a paradigm shift. We are no longer designing static screens; we are designing the rules, constraints, and components that the AI will use to assemble those screens.</p>

        <blockquote>
          "The designer of the future isn't a pixel-pusher, but a system architect. We will define the boundaries of the sandbox, and the AI will build the sandcastles."
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Ethical Considerations in AI Design</h2>
        <p>With great power comes great responsibility. As AI plays a larger role in shaping user experiences, we must remain vigilant against algorithmic bias. If the training data used to generate our UIs is flawed, the resulting designs will inherit those flaws. Dark patterns could be generated and optimized at terrifying speeds.</p>
        <p>Designers must advocate for transparency. Users should understand when they are interacting with a generated interface or AI agent. Furthermore, establishing "human-in-the-loop" fail-safes is critical to ensure that AI doesn't prioritize business metrics (like engagement) over user well-being.</p>

        <h2>Where Designers Fit In</h2>
        <p>So, where does this leave the human designer? Right in the center. Empathy, strategic thinking, and the ability to understand nuanced human emotions remain distinctly human traits. AI can generate a thousand variations of a button, but it cannot understand the frustration a user feels when a workflow is counter-intuitive to their specific cultural context.</p>
        <p>The future belongs to the "Centaur Designer"—the professional who seamlessly integrates AI tools into their workflow to enhance their output, while relying on their innate human intuition to guide the final product. The tools are changing, but the goal remains the same: solving human problems through thoughtful design.</p>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Author Bio -->
    <section class="py-12 bg-[var(--color-bg-subtle)] border-y border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 max-w-[720px]">
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center sm:items-start gap-6 text-center sm:text-left">
          <div class="w-24 h-24 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-[var(--color-primary-light)] text-[var(--color-primary-dark)] flex items-center justify-center text-3xl font-bold font-heading shadow-md">
            S
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text)] font-heading mb-2">Sarah Chen</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--color-text-subtle)] mb-4 leading-relaxed">
              Sarah is the Head of Design at Nexus, with over a decade of experience building digital products. She writes about the intersection of design, technology, and human behavior.
            </p>
            <div class="flex items-center justify-center sm:justify-start gap-4">
              <a href="#" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium text-sm">Follow on Twitter</a>
              <a href="#" class="text-[var(--color-primary)] hover:underline font-medium text-sm">View LinkedIn</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Related Posts -->
    <section class="py-20">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold font-heading text-[var(--color-text)] mb-10 text-center md:text-left">Read Next</h2>
        
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
          {relatedPosts.map(post => (
            <BlogCard {...post} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <CTABanner 
      title="Want more insights? Subscribe to our newsletter." 
      subtitle="Join 10,000+ designers and engineers who get our weekly newsletter."
      primaryCta={{ label: 'Subscribe Now', href: '#' }}
    />
  </main>

  <Footer dark={false} />
</BaseLayout>
